    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The basement rocks of the Red Sea - Gulf of Suez area are dominated by a NW and NS Precambrian fabric which includes foliations, fractures, faults, and dikes. This pre-existing fabric was greatly utilized during the Miocene extension and helped determine the geometries of the basin and the architecture of the Miocene fault system. The N60°W oriented shear zones (Rihba, Queyh, and Hamrawin) have Precambrian left-lateral strike slip component and controlled the locations of accommodation zones at which rift-bounding faults change dip direction. In the Red Sea area the Queyh and Hamrawin shear zones divide the Miocene coastal fault system into three structurally different segments: a southern segment characterized by a NW -NS zig-zag pattern, a central, continuous NW segment with pre-rift strata dipping parallel to the fault (drape folding style), and a northern segment characterized by right-stepping, en-echelon segments and relay ramps. Where the reactivated shear zones, e.g., the Queyh shear, intersect later, ENE faults, they form a depocentre allowing deposition of thick Miocene successions, dominated by fan delta facies. Large-scale NS fault zones, together with the NW shear zones divide the rift into rhomboidal basins of various sizes depending on the length of the Precambrian fabric.\r\n\r\nThe border faults of the rift were generally formed by reactivation of the Precambrian N60W° and NS faults, whereas fault in the sedimentary cover were generally formed perpendicular to Miocene extension. This gives the faults their characteristic zigzag pattern. In the Red Sea, some of the Miocene faults were formed by simultaneous reactivation of NS and NW oriented fault segments.",
